Definition:
The Chain of Responsibility pattern lets you pass a request along a chain of handlers. Each handler decides whether to process the request or pass it to the next handler in the chain.

Pseudocode plan:
1. Use a order processing analogy: Each order has a state (NEW, AMEND, CANCEL).
2. There are specialized handlers: NewOrderHandler, AmendOrderHandler, CancelOrderHandler.
3. Each handler checks if it can process the order based on its state.
4. If it can, it processes the order; if not, it passes the order to the next handler in the chain.
5. If no handler can process the order, it is ignored or logged.

Analogy explanation (order chain only):
1. Imagine a order processing system where every order comes in with a specific state: NEW, AMEND, or CANCEL. There is a chain of handlers, each responsible for one state:

    a. The first handler checks if the order is NEW. If yes, it processes it. If not, it passes the order to the next handler.
    b. The second handler checks if the order is AMEND. If yes, it processes it. If not, it passes the order to the next handler.
    c. The third handler checks if the order is CANCEL. If yes, it processes it. If not, the order is ignored.

2. Each handler only knows how to process its assigned state. If a order doesn’t match, it moves down the chain until a handler processes it or the chain ends.


This is the Chain of Responsibility pattern:
Each handler gets a chance to process the order, and the order moves through the chain until it finds the right handler. This makes the system flexible and easy to extend (add more states/handlers) without changing the existing logic.


public class Order {

    private final String id;
    private final String state;

    public Order(String id, String state) {
        this.id = id;
        this.state = state;
    }

    public String getId() { return id; }

    public String getState() { return state; }
}


public abstract class OrderHandler {

    protected OrderHandler next;

    public void setNext(OrderHandler next) {
        this.next = next;
    }

    public void handle(Order order) {
        if (!process(order) && next != null) {
            next.handle(order);
        } else if (!process(order)) {
            System.out.println(
                "No handler for order: "
                + order.getId()
                + " with state: "
                + order.getState()
            );
        }
    }

    protected abstract boolean process(Order order);
}


public class NewOrderHandler extends OrderHandler {

    @Override
    protected boolean process(Order order) {
        if ("NEW".equalsIgnoreCase(order.getState())) {
            System.out.println("Processing NEW order: " + order.getId());
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}


public class AmendOrderHandler extends OrderHandler {

    @Override
    protected boolean process(Order order) {
        if ("AMEND".equalsIgnoreCase(order.getState())) {
            System.out.println("Processing AMEND order: " + order.getId());
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}



public class CancelOrderHandler extends OrderHandler {

    @Override
    protected boolean process(Order order) {
        if ("CANCEL".equalsIgnoreCase(order.getState())) {
            System.out.println("Processing CANCEL order: " + order.getId());
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}


